<Section title="Principle of Superposition" id="superposition">
</Section>

<SubSection title="Deformation" id="deformation"> 
Superposition: If the displacements are (1) small and (2) linearly related to the force components acting, the displacements caused by the components can be added up:

<DisplayEquation equation='\\delta = \\sum_i \\delta_i = \\sum_i \\frac{F_i L_i}{E_i A_i}\\' title="Superposition." background="True" />

Superposition can also be applied to beam deflection by adding the deflections from different types of loadings.
<Example id="dfl-spp" title="Deflection from a moment and distributed load using superpositon." solution="False">
        <div class="d-flex">
            <p>
                <Image src='/sol/beam_deflection/Superposition Ex.png' width='3'></Image>
              </p>
        </div>
    </Example>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Stress" id="superposition-stress">
If a loading results in more than one type of stress, the total stress in a cross-section can be calculated by adding the individual stresses together (superposition). A review of the stresses covered in this course is below:

<Image src='/sol/combined_loading/Combined_loading_types.png' width='6'></Image>

The goal in combined loading is to determine the stresses at a point in a slender structural member subjected to arbitrary loadings. A cross-section is cut through the point of interest and the internal loading/moments are evaluated at the centroid of the section to maintain equilibrium. This internal system of loading will consist of three force components and three couple vectors (moments). To determine the stress distribution at the point, the principle of superposition is applied.

<Image src='/sol/combined_loading/Superposition.png' width='5'></Image>

<strong>Steps:</strong>
<Enumerate>
	<Item>Cut the beam to find the find internal forces/moments in a section of interest.</Item>
	<Item>Calculate all the stresses acting at a specific point within that section.</Item>
	<Item>Add up like stresses (matching subscripts) to find the total stress state (superposition).</Item>
</Enumerate>

<Example id="com-lds" title="Combined loading in pipe" solution="True">
    <div class="d-flex flex-row">
    <Row>
      <Col values="4 md-4">
        <p>
            <p>
            Find the state of stress at points H and K in the given pipe system. 
            </p>
            <p>
            Assume that <InlineEquation equation = "F_1"/> = 2500 lbs, <InlineEquation equation = "F_2"/> = 600 lbs, and the pipe diameter is 1 inch.
            </p>
            <p>
            Also note that <InlineEquation equation = "a"/> = 2.5 inches, and <InlineEquation equation = "b"/> = 3.5 inches.
            </p>
            <br>
        </p>
      </Col>
      <Col values="8 md-8">
          <Image src='/sol/superposition/Combined Loading Practice.jpg' width='10'></Image>
      </Col>
    </Row>
    </div>
  <div slot="solution">
      <Image src='/sol/superposition/Combined_Loading_Example_FBD.jpg' width='6'>FBD at Cross Section</Image>

      <p>
        First, write all of the six force and moment balance equations.
        </p>

        <p>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{F_x} = V_{x} = 0"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{F_y} = -F_2 + V_{y} = 0"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{F_z} = -F_1 + P_{z} = 0"/>
        </p>
        
        <p>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{M_x} = M_{x} + F_2 (b-1) = 0"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{M_y} = M_{y} = 0"/>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum{M_z} = -T_{z} + F_2 a = 0"/>
        </p>

        <Image src='/sol/superposition/Simplified_FBD.jpg' width='4'>Simplified Free Body Diagram</Image>

        <p>
        Next, compute the relevant stresses produced by each force or moment. Note that for this example, <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma_x"/> and <InlineEquation equation="\\sigma_y"/> would be zero. This is because the normal axis is in the z direction, and there is no internal pressure to create normal stresses in the x or y directions.
        </p>

        <p>
        <strong> For point H: </strong>
        <Itemize>
            <Item> Axial: <InlineEquation equation = "\\sigma_z = \\dfrac{P_z}{A} = \\dfrac{F_1}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4}}" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Shear: <InlineEquation equation = "\\tau_{zy} = \\dfrac{V_y Q}{It} = 0" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Moment: <InlineEquation equation = "\\sigma_z = \\dfrac{Mc}{I} = \\dfrac{F_2(b-1)(r)}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^4}{64}}" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Torsion: <InlineEquation equation = "\\tau_{zx} = \\dfrac{T_z r}{J} = \\dfrac{F_2 (a) (r)}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^4}{32}}" /> </Item>
        </Itemize>
        </p>

        <p>

        <strong> For point K: </strong>
        <Itemize>
            <Item> Axial: <InlineEquation equation = "\\sigma_z = \\dfrac{P_z}{A} = \\dfrac{F_1}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4}}" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Shear: <InlineEquation equation = "\\tau_{zy} = \\dfrac{V_y Q}{It} = \\dfrac{F_2\\dfrac{2 (r^3)}{3}}{(\\dfrac{\\pi \d^4}{64}) (d)}" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Moment: <InlineEquation equation = "\\sigma_z = \\dfrac{Mc}{I} = 0" /> </Item>
            <br></br>
            <Item> Torsion: <InlineEquation equation = "\\tau_{zx} = \\dfrac{T_z r}{J} = \\dfrac{F_2 (a) (r)}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^4}{32}}" /> </Item>
        </Itemize>
        </p>
  </div>
</Example>
